              "textField": "The calcium channel blockers are based on the fact that our blood vessel muscles and the muscles of our heart need calcium in order to contract. Calcium channel blockers include amlodipine, which is a *pregnancy category C* drug, but not used in pregnancy as much as *nifedipine* which is another calcium channel blocker that is a pregnancy category C drug. If a pregnancy category A drug is desired, *methyldopa* is used, which is an alpha 2 AGONIST, however it is associated with several side effects including *psychological* ones, so it is rarely used outside of pregnancy. The ACE inhibitor Ramipril and the angiotensin II receptor blocker *candesartan* are both *pregnancy category D* drugs and as such are contraindicated in pregnancy. The calcium channel blockers may cause *peripheral oedema* in which case a diuretic is used, which normally allows the excess fluid to be excreted."
